      "math_academy_id": "e-12886",
      "problem": "$\\qquad$ $x$ is a multiple of $5$ or $y$ divides $24$.\n\nWhat is the negation of the composite predicate above?",
      "worked_solution": "De Morgan's law for disjunctions states that\n\n$$\n\\neg ( A \\lor B) = \\neg A \\land \\neg B.\n$$\n\nSo, the negation of \"$x$ is a multiple of $5$ or $y$ divides $24$\" is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}¬(x\\text{ is a multiple of [math]5[/math] or }y\\text{ divides }24) & ≡¬(x\\text{ is a multiple of }5∨y\\text{ divides }24) \\\\  & ≡¬(x\\text{ is a multiple of }5)∧¬(y\\text{ divides }24) \\\\  & ≡x\\text{ is not a multiple of }5∧y\\text{ does not divide }24 \\\\  & ≡x\\text{ is not a multiple of }5\\text{ and }y\\text{ does not divide }24.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "html_path": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14118162/observations/step-e12886-067327c31439ecc72bb5.html",
